/* ============================================================================
octal-to-decimal.epher -- octal literals read as decimal values

Evaluates octal literals and prints their decimal values. An octal
literal is the prefix 0o followed by digits 0 to 7, and its value is
the sum of the powers of eight in it: 0o17 = 8 + 7 = 15. The prefix
changes the spelling, never the value, so the results below are plain
decimal numbers.

Source: standard numeral systems; positional notation in base 8, the
place values 1, 8, 64 and 512 made explicit under the first check.
Evaluation is exact for every value here.

Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

There is no knob: the sample literals are the point of the script.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the place values -----------------------------------------------------------------
// 0o777 has 7 in the 64, 8 and 1 places, so it is 7 * 64 + 7 * 8 + 7.
print("7 * 64 + 7 * 8 + 7:", 7 * 64 + 7 * 8 + 7)
print("one eight and seven ones, 15:", 0o17)
print("the largest three-digit octal, 511:", 0o777)
print("8^3 = 512:", 0o1000)
print("five eights and two ones, 42:", 0o52)

// ---- a list at a glance ---------------------------------------------------------------
print("0o1 reads as:", 0o1)
print("0o7 reads as:", 0o7)
print("0o10 reads as:", 0o10)
print("0o17 reads as:", 0o17)
print("0o52 reads as:", 0o52)
print("0o100 reads as:", 0o100)
print("0o777 reads as:", 0o777)
/* ---- expected output ----
= 7 * 64 + 7 * 8 + 7: 511
= one eight and seven ones, 15: 15
= the largest three-digit octal, 511: 511
= 8^3 = 512: 512
= five eights and two ones, 42: 42
= 0o1 reads as: 1
= 0o7 reads as: 7
= 0o10 reads as: 8
= 0o17 reads as: 15
= 0o52 reads as: 42
= 0o100 reads as: 64
= 0o777 reads as: 511
*/